# Summary
The commit fixes the previous fatal lease-expiry crash path for timestamp regressions, but it introduces a new availability deadlock: the forward-jump guard is applied to heartbeats and other timestamped commands, while lease expiry deliberately refuses to commit a catch-up timestamp. Any gap greater than the one-hour window between the stored high-water timestamp and the current authority time can therefore brick the control plane.
This commit adds MAX_COMMITTED_TIMESTAMP_FORWARD_JUMP_MS and rejects any committed command timestamp more than one hour ahead of the previous max_committed_timestamp_ms. It also makes periodic lease expiry return a successful empty result instead of committing when the local time is beyond that bound. Because the persisted high-water timestamp is not advanced on restart, a control plane that was stopped or otherwise did not commit heartbeats for more than one hour will restart with max_committed_timestamp_ms still at the old value. At current wall-clock time, expire_heartbeat_leases() will no-op and will not advance the high-water mark, while storage-node heartbeats still go through RecordNodeHeartbeat and are rejected by validate_committed_timestamp() as CommittedTimestampTooFarAhead. Since heartbeat leases are only 10 seconds, existing leases are already expired, no node can obtain a new serving lease, and the object service remains unavailable until manual state repair or clock manipulation. The same pattern is present in the experimental Raft lease-expiry path.

