## Report
Validated the finding with a targeted Rust PoC and LLDB trace. The changed code introduces MAX_COMMITTED_TIMESTAMP_FORWARD_JUMP_MS=3600000 at crates/storage/src/control_plane.rs:21, rejects timestamps beyond that bound in validate_committed_timestamp at crates/storage/src/control_plane.rs:860-874, persists stale max_committed_timestamp_ms across open/restart at crates/storage/src/control_plane.rs:3558-3572, sends heartbeats through RecordNodeHeartbeat with authority_now_ms at crates/storage/src/control_plane.rs:3720-3751, and returns an empty no-op expiry without ratcheting timestamp at crates/storage/src/control_plane.rs:3775-3788. I built and ran a PoC as a storage example. It established a serving lease at high_water=1001 and lease_deadline=1101, reopened the file-backed control plane, then simulated now=3601124 (> high_water+1h and > lease_deadline). expire_heartbeat_leases(3601124) returned expired_nodes=0 and high_water_after=Some(1001), proving the expiry path did not catch up or expire the stale lease. A subsequent heartbeat at the same authority time failed with: committed timestamp 3601124ms is more than 3600000ms ahead of previous maximum 1001ms. LLDB breakpoints confirmed the chain: breakpoint at control_plane.rs:3783 showed expire_heartbeat_leases returning the empty result with now_ms=3601124; breakpoint at control_plane.rs:869 showed validate_committed_timestamp rejecting the heartbeat with timestamp_ms=3601124 and max_committed_timestamp_ms=1001, reached from SingleAuthorityControlPlane::heartbeat via apply_and_commit_command. Direct crash testing did not produce a crash because this is an availability/liveness bug, not memory corruption. Valgrind was unavailable (`valgrind: command not found`), and Rust ASan via `-Z sanitizer` was unavailable on the stable toolchain. Existing targeted tests also passed and corroborate the constituent behavior: `cargo test -q -p storage committed_timestamp_forward_jump -- --nocapture` and `cargo test -q -p storage expire_heartbeat_leases_proposal_defers_forward_timestamp_jump_without_ratchet -- --nocapture`.

# Resolution
Fixed by commit [fda75348887d410b420a74fa686f46ef81927849](https://github.com/justincormack/argmin/commit/fda75348887d410b420a74fa686f46ef81927849) (`Fix far-forward heartbeat lease expiry recovery`).

The fix keeps the committed timestamp forward-jump guard for timestamp-only proposals, but allows a far-forward `ExpireHeartbeatLeases` command to commit when it actually expires an existing eligible heartbeat lease. That gives the control plane a deterministic recovery step after long downtime: expired leases are marked unavailable, `max_committed_timestamp_ms` advances to the expiry timestamp, and subsequent storage-node heartbeats can renew leases instead of remaining wedged behind `CommittedTimestampTooFarAhead`. The single-authority and experimental Raft wrapper paths now both defer only far-forward expiry calls that would be timestamp-only no-ops. Regression coverage was added for file-backed single-authority downtime recovery and the experimental Raft wrapper recovery path.

Verification for the fix:
- `cargo fmt`
- `cargo clippy --workspace --all-targets --all-features -- -D warnings`
- `cargo nextest run` passed: 6295 tests run, 6295 passed

