## Path evidence
- `crates/storage/src/control_plane.rs:20-21` - Defines MAX_HEARTBEAT_LEASE_MS as 10 seconds and MAX_COMMITTED_TIMESTAMP_FORWARD_JUMP_MS as one hour, creating the liveness boundary.
- `crates/storage/src/control_plane.rs:860-884` - validate_committed_timestamp rejects committed timestamps more than one hour ahead of the previous maximum.
- `crates/storage/src/control_plane.rs:1645-1648` - ExpireHeartbeatLeases commands are also subject to the same committed timestamp validation before they can advance the timestamp.
- `crates/storage/src/control_plane.rs:3558-3572` - Opening persisted control-plane state bumps authority/epoch but does not advance or reset max_committed_timestamp_ms.
- `crates/storage/src/control_plane.rs:3720-3751` - Storage-node heartbeats commit RecordNodeHeartbeat using authority_now_ms, which will be far ahead after long downtime.
- `crates/storage/src/control_plane.rs:3775-3788` - Single-authority lease expiry returns an empty successful result instead of committing a catch-up timestamp when now_ms exceeds the forward bound.
- `crates/argmin-s3/src/main.rs:1373-1435` - The control-plane manager opens persisted state, accepts internal Unix-socket RPCs, and periodically calls expire_heartbeat_leases with current_time_millis.
- `crates/argmin-s3/src/main.rs:1598-1602` - Experimental Raft lease-expiry path has the same no-op behavior when the current timestamp is too far forward.
- `crates/argmin-s3/src/main.rs:2703-2779` - Control-plane sockets are Unix sockets whose parent directory is created or validated as private, limiting direct attacker reachability.
- `crates/argmin-s3/src/main.rs:3429-3505` - The externally exposed S3 service binds the configured TCP listen address and serves HTTP/TLS separately from the internal control-plane socket path.
- `crates/argmin-s3/src/config.rs:135-168` - Configuration shows the public listener defaults to 127.0.0.1:9000 and control-plane paths are separate environment-configured local artifacts.
## Narrative
The code evidence supports a real liveness bug: the commit adds a one-hour committed-timestamp forward bound, persists/reloads control-plane state without advancing the high-water timestamp, makes lease expiry no-op when current time is too far forward, and then rejects storage-node heartbeats using current authority time. The provided validation PoC demonstrated this sequence and showed heartbeat renewal failure. However, the attack path is not an externally reachable S3 API path: the public HTTP listener handles S3 traffic, while the affected heartbeat/control-plane path is internal over private Unix sockets and depends on downtime, restart, or clock conditions. Therefore the original high security severity is not supported; this is primarily an operational availability/reliability defect rather than an attacker-reachable high-severity vulnerability.
## Controls
- S3 HTTP requests use configured credential store and SigV4 authentication outside explicitly public bucket behavior
- control-plane RPC uses configured Unix socket paths rather than a public TCP listener
- control-plane socket parent directory is required to be private and owned by the process uid
- HTTP server supports max connection and max in-flight request limits
- TLS is optional via ARGMIN_TLS_CERT_PATH and ARGMIN_TLS_KEY_PATH
## Blindspots
- Static-only review cannot prove how operators deploy ARGMIN_LISTEN_ADDR, socket paths, filesystem permissions, or process supervisors in production.
- No deployment manifests were analyzed to confirm whether distributed control-plane mode is commonly used or exposed in a particular environment.
- A chained attack where a separate vulnerability lets an attacker stop the control plane, alter clocks, or write control-plane state was not assessed because it is not evidenced by this finding.
- Experimental Raft peer deployment and inter-node transport exposure could not be fully characterized from repository code alone.
